# Rule: Release Stigmer OSS

## Purpose

When triggered, determine the correct semver bump, create annotated tags with detailed release notes, and push them to origin — triggering all downstream CI release pipelines.

## Rule Type

**Action Rule** - Executes git tag and push operations

## When to Use

- When user asks to "release", "cut a release", "tag a release", or "make release"
- After a set of changes on `main` are ready to ship

## Execution Steps

### 1. Identify the Previous Release

```bash
git tag -l "v*" | sort -V | tail -n1
```

Report the latest tag to the user (e.g. "Previous release: **v0.1.2**").

### 2. Collect Commits Since Last Release

```bash
git log --oneline <previous-tag>..HEAD
```

List commits for the user so they can see what's included.

### 3. Determine the Version Bump

Use the **impact-based** semver policy below. The presence of a `feat` commit type alone is NOT sufficient for a minor bump — evaluate the actual scope and impact of the changes.

| Bump | Criteria |
|------|----------|
| **major** | Breaking changes (`BREAKING CHANGE:` footer or `!` after type/scope). Public API contracts change in incompatible ways. Proto field removals or renames. SDK exports removed or signatures changed. |
| **minor** | Significant new capabilities that expand what users or platform builders can do — new API resources, new CLI commands, new major SDK surface areas (e.g., a whole new domain module), new platform-level features visible in the product. The bar: "Would this warrant a section in release notes that makes users excited to upgrade?" |
| **patch** | Everything else: bug fixes, incremental enhancements to existing features (new components within an existing domain, UX improvements, refactors), CI/CD changes, documentation, tooling, chores, performance improvements, new tests. The vast majority of `feat` commits in day-to-day development are patch-level — they enhance existing capabilities rather than introducing new platform-level features. |

**Default to patch.** When in doubt between minor and patch, choose patch. A release can always be followed by another release — being conservative avoids version inflation.

Present the reasoning and proposed new version. Ask the user to confirm before proceeding.

### 4. Check for Uncommitted Changes

```bash
git status --short
```

If there are uncommitted changes, warn the user and ask whether to commit first or abort.

### 5. Compose Release Notes

Build a Markdown-formatted annotated tag message. Start with a user-facing highlights summary, then group commits by Conventional Commit type:

```
Release v<NEW_VERSION>

## Highlights

<1-3 sentence plain-language summary for end users. Explain what this release
improves and why users should upgrade. Do NOT repeat commit messages — write a
narrative. Example: "This release adds real-time shell output streaming during
agent executions and fixes several codegen edge cases that could produce
invalid Go code.">

## What's Changed

### Features
- **<scope>**: <description>

### Bug Fixes
- **<scope>**: <description>

### CI/CD
- **<scope>**: <description>

### Documentation
- **<scope>**: <description>

### Chores
- <description>

**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/stigmer/stigmer/compare/<PREV>...<NEW>
```

Rules for the notes:
- The **Highlights** section is mandatory — it is the first thing users read on the GitHub release page. Write it in plain language for people who use Stigmer, not for contributors scanning commits
- Omit Conventional Commit sections that have no commits (e.g. skip "### Features" if there are no `feat` commits)
- Skip merge commits and project-management docs commits (changelogs, session checkpoints, next-task updates) from the notes — they add noise
- Use imperative mood and keep each bullet to one line
- Include scope in bold when available

### 6. Create Tags

Create three annotated tags (mirroring the `make release` target):

```bash
git tag -a "sdk/go/v<NEW>"     -m "Release sdk/go v<NEW>"
git tag -a "mcp-server/v<NEW>" -m "Release mcp-server v<NEW>"
git tag -a "v<NEW>"            -m "<full release notes from step 5>"
```

The `sdk/go` and `mcp-server` tags get short messages; the main `v*` tag gets the full release notes.

### 7. Push Tags

```bash
for t in "sdk/go/v<NEW>" "mcp-server/v<NEW>" "v<NEW>"; do
  echo "  pushing $t"
  git push origin "$t"
done
```

### 8. Confirm

After pushing, print the CI summary (same as `make release`):

```
Tags pushed. CI will handle:
  - Protos to BSR                  (release.buf.yaml)
  - CLI binaries + GitHub release  (release.cli.yaml)
  - Desktop app installers (draft) (release.desktop.yaml)
  - @stigmer/* npm packages        (release.npm-libs.yaml)
  - Go SDK (go get)                (sdk/go tag auto-cached by proxy.golang.org)
  - stigmer + stigmer-protos PyPI  (release.python-sdk.yaml)
  - MCP server binaries + Docker   (release.mcp-server.yaml)
```

## Important Notes

- **Never force-push tags** — if a tag already exists, stop and tell the user
- **Always confirm** the version bump with the user before creating tags
- **Clean working tree required** — do not tag with uncommitted changes unless the user explicitly approves
- If the user specifies a bump type (`patch`, `minor`, `major`), use it; otherwise infer from commits
